.SH "BUGS"
The condition \fB[ \-f $(get_stderr \|\.\|\.\|\.) ]\fR is unquoted; if the path returned by \fBget_stderr\fR contains spaces, the test will fail or behave unexpectedly\.
.P
\fBget_stderr\fR relies on \fB/proc/<pid>/fd/2\fR, which is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
The glob \fB*out\fR in the interactive phase matches any file ending in \fBout\fR, not just files with the \fB\.out\fR extension \(em for example, \fBstderr_output\fR would also be matched\. The Slurm phase uses \fB*\.out\fR (with a dot), so the two phases are inconsistent\.
.P
If \fBslurm\-outputs/\fR does not exist or contains no \fB*\.out\fR files, the \fBfor\fR loop in phase 2 will iterate once with the literal string \fBslurm\-outputs/*\.out\fR as \fB$file\fR, and \fBcheck_for_errors\fR will be called with that unexpanded glob as its argument, likely producing a \fBgrep\fR error\.
.P
Phase 2 always runs unconditionally after phase 1 completes normally\. There is no way to suppress the Slurm scan, even in environments where \fBslurm\-outputs/\fR is not relevant\.
.P
The interactive prompt does not have a newline after it, so on some terminals the user's input appears on the same line as the prompt\.
